{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>In the day.</b> And the wise man who is a man of property and silver — why should he rejoice in something that will not endure? It is as though he is told: when you are in a good day, be in good and rejoice. Therefore he must take heed, for an evil day will come. Like the matter of 'rejoice, young man' — it tells him: rejoice, and walk in the ways of your heart's desire and your eyes, and know that over all these… And behold, the sense is: do not rejoice." "<b>Also this opposite that.</b> Like the matter stated in the Book of Formation: seven duplicated [letters] bet, gimel, dalet, kaf, pe, resh, tav. And על דברת is like בעבור ('on account of'); it is like על דבר אשר לא קדמו אתכם, and like its parallel על דברתי מלכי צדק על דבר אמת וענוה צדק. And the yod is added, like the yod of מלאתי משפט רבתי בגוים שרתי במדינות, and after it 'he will return to Elohim.' And the sense is that the Maker made this opposite that, and a person cannot lay a claim, on account of [the fact that] the deficiency comes from the recipient, as I explained at the beginning of the book."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {1 "The verse quotations in this comment are heavily truncated and strung together elliptically; rendered hyper-literally without supplying the missing words, as the text appears deliberately compressed."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:28:11.281072788Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}